\begin{abstract}
% Large Language Models (LLMs) have demonstrated strong capabilities in generating source code across multiple programming languages. However, their outputs often exhibit distinctive stylistic and structural traits that differ from human-written code and vary across models. These differences carry concrete practical implications: in practice, security auditing needs motivate code provenance and stylistic analysis for attribution, reusability, and model selection guidance. Existing text-based detection methods face significant performance drops when directly applied to code attribution, due to fundamental differences between natural language and code semantics. To address the inadequacy of these methods, we propose the Code Generation Source Detection (CGSD) framework, which learns discriminative, style-sensitive embeddings via supervised contrastive learning with subsequent classification fine-tuning; similarity analysis and CodeStyleAnalyzer then quantify and interpret stylistic patterns. We conduct experiments on a self-constructed multi-class dataset comprising code generated by multiple mainstream LLMs. Results show that CGSD achieves 93.25\% attribution accuracy, significantly outperforming existing baseline methods, effectively capturing model-specific patterns and uncovering systematic stylistic divergences between models.
Large Language Models (LLMs) have demonstrated exceptional performance in source code generation across multiple programming languages. However, LLM-generated code often suffers from inconsistent style, structural redundancy, and potential security vulnerabilities. Therefore, source attribution for generated code is crucial for identifying the model origin, evaluating quality, and enhancing reliability. Due to strict syntactic constraints, code generated by different models for the same prompt exhibits much higher similarity than natural language text, causing existing text source attribution methods to degrade when applied to code attribution. To address this and analyze stylistic differences, we propose the Code Generation Source Detection (CGSD) framework. It first employs Supervised Contrastive Learning to obtain distinguishable code embeddings and trains a classifier to determine the code's origin. CGSD then combines a similarity analysis module with the code style analyzer to deeply analyze coding styles of different models. Experiments on a self-constructed, multi-class dataset containing code from various mainstream LLMs show that CGSD achieves 93.25\% accuracy, significantly outperforming existing baselines. This confirms that CGSD can both effectively capture model-specific generation patterns and reveal systematic stylistic differences across models.
\end{abstract}

\begin{IEEEkeywords}
Large Language Models, Code Generation, Supervised Contrastive Learning, Stylistic Analysis
\end{IEEEkeywords}

\section{Introduction}

With the rapid advancement of large language models (LLMs) in code generation, output quality now approaches expert-level performance~\citep{Coignion_2024,rasheed2024codepori}, improving productivity across research and industry. Automated coding helps development teams focus on innovation and problem modeling rather than repetitive implementation.

Budget, compute, and expertise constraints push development teams toward cheaper or mixed-model pipelines, increasing exposure to security risks. About 26\% of 452 GitHub Copilot snippets contained security vulnerabilities~\citep{FuCopilot2023}, underscoring the need for code provenance detection and stylistic auditing. Mixed-model codebases also suffer from divergent conventions—for instance, ChatGPT favors \texttt{number} while Gemini prefers \texttt{n}—which disrupts reuse and integration, motivating systematic source attribution.

Existing AIGC detection work has focused on detecting machine-generated text and code~\citep{yangZeroShotDetectionMachineGenerated2023,baoFastDetectGPTEfficientZeroShot2023,mitchellDetectGPTZeroShotMachineGenerated2023}, but these efforts still fall short of fine-grained attribution across different generative models. Although some studies report strong results for text-level classification~\citep{sunIdiosyncrasiesLargeLanguage2025}, applying those text-based classifiers directly to code leads to a marked drop in performance. LLM-generated programs are far more lexically and structurally homogeneous than natural language, so different model outputs often overlap in embedding space and are hard to separate. Moreover, code contains rich structural and semantic signals — syntax and control-flow patterns, domain-specific idioms, and stylistic markers in naming, comments, and layout — that general text models fail to capture well. These facts motivate domain-specific adaptations that enlarge inter-source margins and explicitly model code’s structural and stylistic features for reliable source attribution.

